          "case_name": "Ramsey v. Yavapai Family Advocacy Center",
          "citation": "225 Ariz. 132, 235 P.3d 285 (App. 2010)",
          "court": "Court of Appeals of Arizona",
          "year": 2010,
          "date_decided": "2010-07-13",
          "holding": "The court of appeals affirmed summary judgment for the reporter and the forensic interviewers, holding that A.R.S. § 13-3620(J) confers qualified immunity conditioned on the initial reporter's reasonable belief that abuse occurred and that a counselor owes no duty to a third-party alleged abuser.",
          "relevance": "Bears on liability exposure when a report is later unfounded or screened out.",
          "quote": "as the initial reporter of the alleged abuse, Sheets enjoys qualified immunity under the statute only if she \"reasonably believe[d]\" abuse had occurred",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/2630890/ramsey-v-yavapai-family-advocacy-center/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 2630890
        },
        {
          "case_name": "L.A.R. v. Ludwig",
          "citation": "170 Ariz. 24, 821 P.2d 291 (App. 1991)",
          "court": "Court of Appeals of Arizona",
          "year": 1991,
          "date_decided": "1991-11-21",
          "holding": "The court of appeals held that a person with reasonable grounds to report suspected child abuse has qualified statutory immunity defeasible only by proof of malice, but reversed and remanded because the immunity ruling did not dispose of the plaintiffs' separate negligent-treatment and consortium claims.",
          "relevance": "Shows that an ultimately unfounded report does not by itself expose the reporter to liability.",
          "quote": "Malicious intent must be proved by the plaintiff to defeat the defense of qualified immunity.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/1238265/lar-v-ludwig/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 1238265
        },
        {
          "case_name": "Lorenz v. State",
          "citation": "238 Ariz. 556, 364 P.3d 475 (App. 2015)",
          "court": "Court of Appeals of Arizona",
          "year": 2015,
          "date_decided": "2015-12-08",
          "holding": "The court of appeals affirmed dismissal of grandparents' civil suit against DCS, holding the child-welfare statutes, rules, and policies they invoked create no civil duty running to relatives who seek placement of a dependent child.",
          "relevance": "Shows that an agency's decision not to act on a relative's information is not privately actionable.",
          "quote": "Because the cited authorities reveal an intent to protect and benefit dependent children, not potential foster or adoptive placements, we affirm the superior court's judgment.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/3160631/lorenz-v-state/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 3160631
        },
        {
          "case_name": "DeShaney v. Winnebago County Department of Social Services",
          "citation": "489 U.S. 189 (1989)",
          "court": "Supreme Court of the United States",
          "year": 1989,
          "date_decided": "1989-02-22",
          "holding": "The Supreme Court affirmed, holding that the Due Process Clause imposed no affirmative constitutional duty on county caseworkers to protect a child from his father's violence, because the State had not restrained the child's liberty and so had no custodial special relationship with him.",
          "relevance": "The controlling federal answer to the parent's or relative's claim that the agency's failure to act on a report is itself unconstitutional — knowledge of danger alone creates no duty.",
          "quote": "As a general matter, then, we conclude that a State's failure to protect an individual against private violence simply does not constitute a violation of the Due Process Clause",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/112202/deshaney-v-winnebago-county-department-of-social-services/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 112202
        }
